# MenuMesh's lines for the server's /srv/edge/edge.env (root, 0600): the values menumesh.caddy
# reads as {$MENUMESH_*}. app-apply accepts these names only once /etc/apps/menumesh.conf exists
# (a name must start with a configured app's prefix), and installs the file it is given in place
# of the live one. So the server's admin copies the live file under umask 077 and gives its last
# line a line end if it has none, so that the first line added can't join the last value,
#   (umask 077; sudo cat /srv/edge/edge.env > ~/incoming/edge.env)
#   [ -z "$(tail -c1 ~/incoming/edge.env)" ] || echo >> ~/incoming/edge.env
# pastes the two lines below, with the owner's hash, into cat >> ~/incoming/edge.env, which expands
# nothing of the hash's $, checks the names and lengths without printing a value (each name once,
# none (0), the user (8) and the hash (62): 60 characters and two quotes), installs the copy with
#   sudo app-apply edge env ~/incoming/edge.env && shred -u ~/incoming/edge.env
# and recreates Caddy between 01:00 and 07:00 IST (sudo app-compose edge up -d), which reads
# edge.env only when it is created. docs/RUNBOOK.md, First install, step 11, has every command and
# what each must show. Values never in git: the password and its hash live in the owner's password
# manager.

# Staging's basic auth: one account. The owner makes the hash with the edge's pinned Caddy image on
# their own machine, never on the server, where the password would stay in the shell's history and
# show in the process list. Without --plaintext, Caddy reads it from the terminal, not echoed:
#   docker run --rm -it caddy:2.11.9-alpine@sha256:003a97808e781aa9f7e75cc2f43d34d461099031faf5e20faefb9f61126836f0 caddy hash-password --algorithm bcrypt
# The hash is bcrypt, 60 characters from $2a$: app-apply edge env refuses anything else. The admin
# gets only the hash. Keep the single quotes: the hash holds $ signs.
MENUMESH_STAGING_AUTH_USER=menumesh
MENUMESH_STAGING_AUTH_HASH='$2a$14$replace-me'
